{"episodes":{"211951":"A cluster of strong thunderstorms developed into northwestern Arkansas during the early morning hours of the 5th, the strongest of which produced hail up to penny size.","211991":"A deep surface low pressure system was positioned north of the Great Lakes on the evening of May 4th, with an associated cold front pushing into and across central Illinois. The airmass ahead of the front was characterized by moderate instability and steep mid-level lapse rates, which supported large hail. Two areas of thunderstorms formed, the first being isolated supercells that tracked near and south of I-70 well ahead of the cold front. The other area was a broken line of storms that developed along the front and advanced southward across the region during the late evening. Both sets of storms produced large hail, the largest of which was ping pong ball-sized in Clay County.","212435":"Isolated to scattered severe thunderstorms develop along a southward moving cold front through the evening bringing strong wind gusts to southeast WI.","212650":"Scattered thunderstorms developed during the early morning hours of May 5th, 2026. These thunderstorms gradually moved east throughout the night, producing a few reports of quarter to half dollar sized hail across south-central Kansas.","212795":"Scattered thunderstorms moved across parts of northern Illinois during the late afternoon and evening of May 4th producing isolated severe wind gusts.","212804":"A small bowing cluster produced isolated damaging wind across east-central KS around and shortly after midnight on May 5th.","213120":"During the afternoon of May 4th, a cold front was approaching the region from the northwest. As the low-level jet ramped up in the late afternoon, convection blossomed. One thunderstorm was able to become severe and produce large hail in Madison and Bond counties.","213144":"Breezy conditions on the afternoon on May 4 resulted in instances of zero to near-zero visibility as soil and dust was stirred up in northwest Indiana. Later on, isolated severe thunderstorms produced small hail and gusty wind.","213227":"Warm southwesterly flow ahead of a cold front, combined with ample lift and moisture, helped trigger scattered showers and thunderstorms Tuesday afternoon. Steep low-level temperature lapse rates and instability allowed for thunderstorm development. Although modeled wind shear was not particularly strong, it was sufficient to organize some storms into clusters, resulting in gusty to locally damaging winds.","213501":"Scattered thunderstorms developed along a front that was moving across the area during the morning afternoon hours on May 4th.  These storms produced gusts of 40 to 50 MPH but a few of these storms became severe and produced hail to 1 inch in diameter and damaging straight-line winds.","213503":"Scattered thunderstorms developed along a front that was moving across the area during the morning afternoon hours on May 4th.  These storms produced gusts of 40 to 50 MPH but a few of these storms became severe and produced hail to 1 inch in diameter and damaging straight-line winds.","213574":"A few severe thunderstorms producing wind damage developed over Macomb, St. Clair, and Wayne counties.","213576":"Thunderstorms moving through Lake St. Clair produced wind gusts in excess of 45 knots.","214282":"A thunderstorm moving through Saginaw Bay produced a wind gusts in excess of 45 knots.","214369":"On May 4th, storms brought large hail and damaging winds to portions of Missouri."},"events":[{"begin":"2026-05-05T00:00Z","county":"Henderson","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-05-05T00:00Z","endLat":40.94,"endLon":-90.85,"episode":213503,"id":1337439,"injuries":0,"lat":40.94,"location":"1.25 ESE Rozetta","lon":-90.85,"magnitude":53.0,"magnitudeType":"MG","narrative":"A trained spotter reported a 53 knot gusts on a Davis Weather Station.","source":"Trained Spotter","state":"IL","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo"